Spondylolisthesis
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Extension-related back pain, Palpable step-off defect, Hamstring tightness

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-059
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of deep, highly localized lower back pain with a sudden sensation of the spine "giving way," rendering the patient unable to stand.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing an acute, paralyzing flare of severe lumbosacral pain, marked by a terrifying sensation of spinal instability and catching. Urgent physical assessment was strictly required to exclude an acute, unstable vertebral fracture, evaluate for acute foraminal nerve root compression, and provide immediate mechanical stabilization and potent analgesia to restore basic functional safety prior to daytime imaging.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I leaned backward to stretch, felt a sickening shift in my lower spine, and now the pain is so deep and sharp I am terrified to move."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute, severe pain crisis localized to the lumbosacral junction. Timeline: Insidious history of chronic, dull back aching predictably exacerbated by prolonged standing. Two hours ago, while performing a lumbar extension movement, the patient experienced an unpredictable sensation of the lower back suddenly giving way, shifting, and catching. This immediately triggered deep, highly localized, agonizing pain at the L5/S1 level. The pain is massively exacerbated by any attempt to stand in a lordotic posture. Complete relief of the acute, sharp symptoms is achieved solely by resting in a supine position with the knees bent. Patient reports pronounced, painful tightness in the hamstring musculature. Denies radiating pain below the knees, numbness, or bowel/bladder changes.
Functional Status: Acutely bedbound; completely refusing to load the spine vertically due to fear of further structural shifting and pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound distress regarding perceived spinal instability.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 142/88 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, diaphoretic, lying rigidly supine with hips and knees flexed over pillows.
Physical Exam: Inspection of the lower back reveals an exaggerated, compensatory hyperlordotic posture when the patient briefly attempts to stand. Careful, deep palpation reveals a distinct, physical "step-off" defect discovered over the spinous processes at the L5/S1 junction, indicating anterior translation of the vertebral body. Exquisite, deep mechanical pain is elicited at this exact location. Neurological examination is intact: 5/5 motor strength in the lower extremities, normal symmetrical reflexes, and no dermatomal sensory loss. Straight leg raise is significantly restricted bilaterally by severe hamstring tightness (at 40 degrees), but does not produce true shooting radicular nerve pain.
Environmental Observations: N/A
Medication Reconciliation: Took Ibuprofen 400mg with zero relief of the deep mechanical instability pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute symptomatic flare of Lumbar Spondylolisthesis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Vertebral Fracture (e.g., Pars Interarticularis fracture) - Highly suspected as a component of the instability; plain radiographs are absolutely mandatory to assess structural integrity.

Lumbar Disc Herniation - Ruled out by the classic mechanical extension-related pain (disc pain is typically worse with flexion), the palpable step-off defect, and the absence of true radicular nerve shooting pain.

Cauda Equina Syndrome - Ruled out definitively by intact distal neurology, normal reflexes, and absence of sphincter or saddle sensory dysfunction.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, immobilizing mechanical pain crisis characterized by a palpable step-off defect and a history of extension-related pain, classic for spondylolisthesis. The sudden sensation of the spine "giving way" generated intense fear and functional paralysis. The critical clinical task was to carefully assess neurological function to rule out acute catastrophic cord/root compression caused by the slipped vertebra. Examination confirmed the absence of acute hard neurological deficits, isolating the crisis to severe mechanical instability and paraspinal spasm. Delaying evaluation would have left the patient locked in fear and severe pain. Urgent after-hours assessment safely provided crucial diagnostic reassurance, ruled out a neurological emergency, and initiated rigid mechanical rest and analgesia.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Intramuscular Ketorolac 30mg STAT to rapidly reduce deep structural inflammation and capsular pain. Prescribed a short 3-day course of oral Diazepam 2mg BD PRN to explicitly break the severe, protective hamstring and paraspinal muscle spasms.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Rest strictly on your back with your calves elevated on a chair or thick stack of pillows, bringing your hips and knees to a 90-degree angle. Consequence: Complete flattening of the lower back physically reverses the painful forward slippage of the bone, instantly switching off the sharp, agonizing "catching" pain (negative reinforcement).
Action: Roll out of bed completely sideways like a log, avoiding any twisting or arching of the back. Consequence: Prevention of the bones grinding across the unstable joint, allowing you to get up to use the bathroom without triggering a severe pain spasm (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 24-48 hours for lateral weight-bearing plain radiographs to quantify the degree of vertebral slippage and refer to an orthopedic spine specialist.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is sudden onset of total numbness in the groin/saddle area, loss of bowel or bladder control, or new, severe shooting pain and weakness traveling down the legs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis strongly suspects a slipped vertebra based on the physical exam. X-rays are absolutely required to measure the exact severity of the slippage. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute, locking spasms will ease with medication and strict rest in the flexed position over 48 hours. Long-term management requires intensive core strengthening to artificially stabilize the spine, or surgical fusion if the slip is severe.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call an ambulance immediately if you lose the ability to control your bladder or bowels, or if your legs suddenly become completely numb or weak.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: One of the bones in your lower spine has slipped slightly forward over the one below it. The sharp pain and "giving way" feeling is mechanical instability. Your body has thrown your hamstrings and back muscles into a massive, defensive spasm to try and hold the spine together. The Valium forces those cramped muscles to let go, while the flat-back resting position takes the physical pressure off the slipped bone.
Actionable Steps: Do not arch your back or bend backward under any circumstances. Rest with your knees bent up high. You must see your regular doctor immediately to organize a standing X-ray of your spine.

